menuconfig PIGEON
  bool "Pigeon Client Platform Library"
  default n
  select REBOOT
  help
    Enable the client-side modeling and synchronization logic
    for linking physical assets to the PidgeIoT edge core.

if PIGEON

choice PIGEON_CONNECTOR_TYPE
  prompt "Default Network Serialization Connector"
  default PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP

config PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP
  bool "CoAP over NIDD/Cellular"
  select COAP
  select JSON_LIBRARY
  help
    Serialize device shadow deltas into minimal byte blocks optimized for
    constrained cellular paths. The wire transport underneath is its own
    choice (PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT below): CoAP-over-DTLS/UDP (RFC 7252,
    coaps://) for constrained/battery/cellular devices, or
    CoAP-over-TLS/TCP (RFC 8323, coaps+tcp://) where a stream socket is a
    better fit.

config PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS
  bool "HTTPS REST API"
  select HTTP_CLIENT
  select JSON_LIBRARY
  # Verifying the platform's chain in software costs 17.2 s on an ESP32-C6
  # without this and 2.8 s with it, and the image comes out 32 bytes smaller.
  # The chain is three P-384 ECDSA signatures, and this is what makes
  # reduction modulo the NIST primes cheap rather than generic bignum work.
  # Implied rather than selected because the symbol needs Zephyr's own PSA
  # ECC, which a board that offloads TLS to a modem never configures; there
  # the imply finds nothing to turn on and the build is unaffected.
  imply MBEDTLS_ECP_NIST_OPTIM
  help
    Standard TLS socket interface for line-powered gateways.

config PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT
  bool "MQTT over TLS"
  select MQTT_LIB
  select MQTT_LIB_TLS
  select JSON_LIBRARY
  select NET_CONTEXT_SNDTIMEO
  help
    One persistent MQTT session to the pigeonhole broker (PidgeIoT's MQTT
    terminator, mqtts://<host>:8883), which bridges every publish onto the
    same device routes the HTTPS connector calls directly, carrying this
    pigeon's own bearer token -- so the broker is never a trusted proxy in
    the authorization sense.

    Topics are session-scoped and carry no pigeon id, because the
    handshake already bound the connection to exactly one pigeon: the
    device publishes pigeon/telemetry, pigeon/shadow/report and
    pigeon/logs, and subscribes to the retained pigeon/shadow/target.
    That retained target REPLACES the shadow poll -- pigeon_shadow_get()
    serves the value the broker last pushed rather than issuing a request
    of its own, and a dashboard write arrives unprompted.

    Zephyr's client speaks MQTT 3.1.1 by default and marks 5.0
    EXPERIMENTAL, so this connector stays on whichever
    CONFIG_MQTT_VERSION_* the build selects; the broker serves both.
    CONFIG_MQTT_KEEPALIVE is the session keepalive and wants a deliberate
    per-target value rather than its 60s default: that suits a mains/WiFi
    device and is wrong for one that sleeps between reports (the broker
    honors up to 30 minutes and closes after 1.5x silence).

    Authentication takes one of the two shapes below (PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH),
    and neither is a transport boundary: any pigeon's token works in
    certificate mode and any PSK-bearing pigeon's PSK completes the
    handshake, exactly as a CoAP pigeon's token already works on every
    device route.

config PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD
  bool "Non-IP Data Delivery through the carrier (nRF91, NB-IoT)"
  depends on NRF_MODEM_LIB
  # Selected, not depended on: through the modem's network interface, which selects it,
  # LTE_LINK_CONTROL leads back to this choice, and Kconfig refuses the loop.
  select LTE_LINK_CONTROL
  select LTE_LC_PDN_MODULE
  select LTE_LC_PSM_MODULE
  select LTE_LC_EDRX_MODULE
  # The module is what writes AT%RAI at modem init; SO_RAI does nothing without it.
  select LTE_LC_RAI_MODULE
  select PSA_CRYPTO
  select PSA_WANT_ALG_HMAC
  select PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_256
  select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_HMAC
  # Implied so an app can still turn it off; with the module in and this off, lte_lc
  # writes AT%RAI=0 at every modem init.
  imply LTE_RAI_REQ
  help
    Frames on a raw socket over a Non-IP PDN, which the carrier hands to ThingSpace and
    ThingSpace posts to the platform. No TLS and no token: the SIM authenticates the device
    to the carrier, and CONFIG_PIGEON_NIDD_CLAIM_KEY binds it to its pigeon and verifies
    every frame the platform sends. docs/api.md in the pidgeiot repository ("NIDD frames")
    is the authority on the frames.

    The application sets the power parameters: an NB-IoT network mode (checked at build
    time), CONFIG_LTE_PSM_REQ_RPTAU (Verizon NB-IoT refused NCS's 30-minute default and
    accepted "00010011", 190 minutes), and eDRX off or shorter than the granted active
    time. The library requests PSM and writes eDRX at every pigeon_init(), because the
    modem keeps both across images.

endchoice

if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP

choice PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT
  prompt "CoAP transport"
  default PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_TCP

config PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_TCP
  bool "CoAP over TLS/TCP (RFC 8323, coaps+tcp://)"
  help
    One TLS/TCP connection per exchange, RFC 8323 framing (no message IDs
    or CoAP-layer retransmission -- TCP owns reliability).
    CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT must be a coaps+tcp:// URL. The board must
    provide a TCP-capable TLS stack (native mbedTLS or an offloading
    modem), same as the HTTPS connector.

config PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_UDP
  bool "CoAP over DTLS/UDP (RFC 7252, coaps://)"
  help
    The primary transport for constrained battery/cellular devices: a
    single long-lived DTLS session (PSK, TLS_PSK_WITH_AES_128_CCM_8
    target) carrying proper RFC 7252 confirmable exchanges -- CON/ACK,
    exponential-backoff retransmission, and duplicate detection, governed
    by Zephyr's own CoAP transmission Kconfigs
    (CONFIG_COAP_INIT_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS, CONFIG_COAP_ACK_RANDOM_PERCENT,
    CONFIG_COAP_BACKOFF_PERCENT, CONFIG_COAP_MAX_RETRANSMIT -- RFC 7252
    sec 4.2's ACK_TIMEOUT/ACK_RANDOM_FACTOR/MAX_RETRANSMIT), not new
    PIGEON-specific knobs. CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT must be a coaps:// URL.

    The board must provide a DTLS-capable stack: natively,
    CONFIG_NET_SOCKETS_ENABLE_DTLS plus a PSK key exchange
    (CONFIG_MBEDTLS_KEY_EXCHANGE_PSK_ENABLED and the AES-CCM PSA wants --
    see the coap_dtls_init sample's board configs in pigeon-examples); on
    nRF91-class boards the modem's own offloaded DTLS is used instead and
    none of the mbedTLS options apply. The DTLS socket is kept open across
    exchanges (unlike the TCP transport's connect-per-exchange): a
    handshake is paid once, then each report rides the established
    session, with an automatic re-handshake if the session dies. See
    PIGEON_COAP_DTLS_CID below for surviving NAT rebinds/PSM sleep without
    even that re-handshake.

endchoice

if PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_UDP

config PIGEON_COAP_DTLS_CID
  bool "Request DTLS Connection ID (RFC 9146) on the CoAP DTLS session"
  default y
  help
    Offers the DTLS Connection ID extension as a client (an empty CID:
    "I'll echo yours, don't send me one" -- TLS_DTLS_CID_SUPPORTED), so a
    CID-capable server can keep attributing this device's records to the
    established session after the source address/port changes underneath
    it. That is exactly what happens to a cellular device behind carrier
    NAT after a PSM sleep: without CID the first post-sleep send from a
    rebound NAT port is unattributable and forces a full re-handshake;
    with CID the same session simply keeps working.

    On by default now that the PidgeIoT CoAP terminator negotiates CID
    server-side (loft's mbedTLS DTLS listener --
    docs/infra/coap-cid-design.md in the pidgeiot repo is the decision
    record). An un-negotiated offer is a no-op on the wire, so builds
    pointed at an older or third-party server keep working unchanged;
    disable only to shave the extension bytes from the ClientHello on a
    deployment whose server will never speak CID.

    Device-side support: native Zephyr sockets wire this into mbedTLS's
    own RFC 9146 support (CONFIG_MBEDTLS_SSL_DTLS_CONNECTION_ID must be
    enabled, or the setsockopt fails and this library logs a warning and
    continues without CID); nRF91 offloaded sockets map the same
    TLS_DTLS_CID option onto the modem's NRF_SO_SEC_DTLS_CID, which
    requires modem firmware mfw_nrf9160 v1.3.5+ or any
    mfw_nrf91x1/mfw_nrf9151-ntn release -- on older firmware the option
    fails and the session continues CID-less, again with a logged
    warning. Either way the negotiated status is logged after each
    handshake (TLS_DTLS_CID_STATUS), so "did CID actually engage" is
    observable per connection rather than assumed.

    Without CID, a wake after a NAT-mapping loss costs one fresh PSK
    handshake before the first request: 3 round trips against a server
    doing RFC 6347 cookie exchange (2 without), ~650 bytes of handshake
    traffic with these PSK suites.

endif # PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_UDP

config PIGEON_COAP_TLS_PSK_IDENTITY
  string "CoAP connector DTLS/TLS PSK identity"
  default ""
  help
    Same build-time-Kconfig-string convention as CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT/
    CONFIG_PIGEON_TOKEN, so a real device's PSK never sits as a literal in
    application source -- it belongs only in a git-ignored prj.local.conf
    fragment. Deliberately NOT read by this library itself, though:
    struct pigeon_coap_config's tls_psk_identity/tls_psk_secret (pigeon.h)
    stay the only thing pigeon_init() actually consults, since the CoAP
    PSK is optional per-device and that struct's NULL-when-absent
    semantics (Option<String>::None, matching capsules::CoapConfig) would
    otherwise have no way to represent "not supplied" against an always-
    defined Kconfig string. An application wires this in itself, and must
    map the empty default to NULL rather than passing it through verbatim
    (see the coap_dtls_init sample's PSK_CONF_OR_NULL): to pigeon_init()
    a non-NULL empty string is a supplied zero-length credential that
    fails every handshake, not an absent one. Conventionally the pigeon
    id, matching capsules::CoapConfig's tls_psk_identity.

config PIGEON_COAP_TLS_PSK_SECRET
  string "CoAP connector DTLS/TLS PSK secret"
  default ""
  help
    Sibling of CONFIG_PIGEON_COAP_TLS_PSK_IDENTITY above -- same
    rationale, same app-wires-it-in-itself convention. Holds the short
    key minted alongside the device's bearer token
    (capsules::CoapConfig's tls_psk_secret), not the token itself.

config PIGEON_COAP_SEC_TAG
  int "TLS sec_tag for the Pigeon CoAP connector"
  default 1
  help
    Security tag under which credentials for CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT are
    provisioned: PSK identity/secret (tls_credential_add, registered
    automatically by this library from pigeon_coap_config when the app
    supplies them via pigeon_init()) or, if the app didn't supply PSK
    fields, a CA cert the app provisioned itself -- same model as
    CONFIG_PIGEON_HTTPS_SEC_TAG. Must match whatever the application
    provisioned under this tag.

endif # PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP

# Also live on an MQTT or NIDD build that carries FOTA: the image fetch is an
# ordinary HTTPS request served by pigeon_https.c, so it needs the same
# socket knobs even though the connector itself is somewhere else.
if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS || ((PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD) && PIGEON_FOTA)

config PIGEON_HTTPS_SEC_TAG
  int "TLS sec_tag for the Pigeon HTTPS requests"
  default 1
  help
    Security tag under which the CA cert for the HTTPS endpoint is
    provisioned (via modem_key_mgmt on nRF91 offloaded sockets, or
    tls_credential_add elsewhere). Must match whatever the application
    provisioned its cert(s) under -- this library does not provision
    anything itself, only opens sockets against this tag.

    On an MQTT build with CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA this is the tag for the
    firmware host named by CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA_HTTPS_ENDPOINT, which is a
    different server from the broker and therefore usually a different tag
    from CONFIG_PIGEON_MQTT_SEC_TAG -- and necessarily so in PSK mode,
    where that tag holds a pre-shared key rather than a CA certificate.
    On a NIDD build with CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA it is the tag for that same
    firmware host, reached over the IP PDN beside the Non-IP one.

config PIGEON_HTTPS_NATIVE_TLS
  bool "Run TLS natively (mbedTLS) over an offloaded transport socket"
  default n
  help
    Adds SOCK_NATIVE_TLS to the connector's socket type, so TLS runs in
    Zephyr's own mbedTLS stack over an offloaded (e.g. nRF91 modem) TCP
    socket instead of inside the modem. Required on boards that keep
    CONFIG_MODEM_KEY_MGMT off and provision their CA certs with
    tls_credential_add() into Zephyr's native credential store: without
    this flag, an offloaded-socket build routes IPPROTO_TLS_1_2 straight
    to the modem's own TLS (see nrf9x_socket_create() in
    nrf/lib/nrf_modem_lib/nrf9x_sockets.c), which looks up
    CONFIG_PIGEON_HTTPS_SEC_TAG in the MODEM's credential store -- empty
    on such boards, so every handshake fails even though the same tag
    works fine for the app's own SOCK_NATIVE_TLS sockets. SOCK_NATIVE_TLS
    is an nRF Connect SDK extension (zephyr/include/zephyr/net/
    socket_ncs.h in the NCS Zephyr fork), so this option is only
    meaningful -- and only compiles -- under an NCS workspace; leave it
    off (the default) everywhere else, including on offloaded-socket
    boards that DO provision the modem store via modem_key_mgmt (the
    hardware-verified https_init configuration). Applies to every HTTPS
    connector socket: shadow, telemetry, log upload, and FOTA downloads.
    CONFIG_PIGEON_WS opens its own sockets and does not honor this yet.

endif # PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS || ((PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD) && PIGEON_FOTA)

if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT

choice PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH
  prompt "MQTT session authentication"
  default PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_CERT

config PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_CERT
  bool "Server certificate and bearer token"
  # The other mode that verifies a chain in software; the HTTPS connector
  # above carries what this is worth and why it is an imply.
  imply MBEDTLS_ECP_NIST_OPTIM
  help
    The shape every off-the-shelf MQTT client supports, and the one for
    mains/WiFi devices: the broker serves a certificate chain the device
    verifies against a CA the application provisioned under
    CONFIG_PIGEON_MQTT_SEC_TAG, then CONNECT carries username =
    pigeon_config.device_id (the pigeon id -- the token has no subject
    claim of its own, so the broker learns which pigeon this is from the
    username) and password = CONFIG_PIGEON_TOKEN. Peer verification is
    required and the hostname is set, so a broker reached by a name its
    certificate does not carry is refused rather than trusted.

    The platform's broker pins an all-ECDSA chain (a P-256 leaf under an
    ECDSA intermediate anchored at ISRG Root X2), so a constrained build
    needs P-256 and P-384 verification only -- no RSA.

config PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_PSK
  bool "TLS-PSK"
  help
    The constrained path, and the one with no CA store and no clock to
    keep: identity = the pigeon id, key = the short secret minted
    alongside the bearer token. This library registers the pair under
    CONFIG_PIGEON_MQTT_SEC_TAG from pigeon_init()'s config through the
    same helper the CoAP connector uses, including its modem-store branch
    on CONFIG_MODEM_KEY_MGMT boards (where credentials must be written
    while the modem is offline, so pigeon_init() has to run BEFORE the
    link comes up).

    No bearer token rides the session at all: the broker resolves this
    pigeon's token server-side from the handshake identity, the same
    arrangement CoAP already has, which is why CONFIG_PIGEON_TOKEN may be
    left empty on a PSK build.

endchoice

config PIGEON_MQTT_SEC_TAG
  int "TLS sec_tag for the Pigeon MQTT connector"
  default 1
  help
    Security tag the session's credentials live under: the PSK identity
    and secret this library registers itself (PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_PSK), or
    the CA certificate the application provisioned (PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_CERT
    -- this library provisions nothing there, it only names the tag).
    Must match whatever the application provisioned under it.

if PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_PSK

config PIGEON_MQTT_TLS_PSK_SECRET
  string "MQTT connector TLS-PSK secret"
  default ""
  help
    The short key minted alongside the device's bearer token
    (capsules::MqttConfig's tls_psk_secret), never the token itself. Same
    build-time-Kconfig-string convention as CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT, so a
    real device's PSK never sits as a literal in application source -- it
    belongs only in a git-ignored prj.local.conf fragment.

    Deliberately NOT read by this library, the same catch the CoAP pair
    carries: struct pigeon_mqtt_config's tls_psk_secret (pigeon.h) is what
    pigeon_init() consults, since the PSK is optional per-device and that
    struct's NULL-when-absent semantics (Option<String>::None, matching
    capsules::MqttConfig) have no way to represent "not supplied" against
    an always-defined Kconfig string. An application wires this in itself
    and must map the empty default to NULL rather than passing it through
    verbatim (see the mqtt_init sample's PSK_CONF_OR_NULL): to
    pigeon_init() a non-NULL empty string is a supplied zero-length
    credential that fails every handshake, not an absent one.

    There is deliberately no matching identity symbol, unlike the CoAP
    connector's pair. A PSK identity here is always the pigeon id, which
    the application already supplies as pigeon_config.device_id, and the
    broker refuses a session whose identity, username and client id
    disagree -- a second place to write the same string could only ever be
    a way to get it wrong.

endif # PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_PSK

config PIGEON_MQTT_TELEMETRY_QOS1
  bool "Publish telemetry at QoS 1"
  default n
  help
    The device chooses the quality of service its telemetry gets, and the
    two are genuinely different paths at the broker rather than two names
    for one. QoS 0 rides the broker's already-open device WebSocket as one
    frame: no ack, one synchronous upsert at the pigeon's Durable Object,
    the cheapest report the platform serves. QoS 1 costs a PUBACK round
    trip and is answered only once the platform has taken the report, so a
    flush that returns 0 means the report landed.

    Consequence worth knowing before mixing them, measured live on the
    platform: the two paths do not share ordering. A QoS 0 report
    published AFTER a QoS 1 one can be stored first, because the frame
    path is a synchronous write and the QoS 1 path is a queued one.
    Ordering holds within a class, not across them.

    Shadow reports and log chunks are always QoS 1 regardless of this
    option: an ack there is the only evidence the write happened.

config PIGEON_MQTT_RX_BUF_SIZE
  int "MQTT receive buffer size (bytes)"
  default 512
  help
    Zephyr's client uses this for packet headers only -- a PUBLISH
    payload is read separately (mqtt_readall_publish_payload), into this
    connector's own buffer sized from CONFIG_PIGEON_SHADOW_CONFIG_MAX.
    512 covers the longest topic here (pigeon/shadow/target) with room
    for MQTT 5 properties.

config PIGEON_MQTT_TX_BUF_SIZE
  int "MQTT transmit buffer size (bytes)"
  default 512
  help
    Header and topic only, for the same reason: mqtt_publish() sends the
    payload straight from the caller's buffer as a second iovec rather
    than copying it here, so a 16KiB log chunk does not need a 16KiB
    transmit buffer.

config PIGEON_MQTT_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
  int "MQTT worker thread stack size (bytes)"
  default 8192
  help
    The worker performs the TLS handshake (mbedTLS) itself on boards that
    do not offload it; 8192 is the same TLS-proven figure
    CONFIG_PIGEON_WS_THREAD_STACK_SIZE uses.

config PIGEON_MQTT_SHADOW_WAIT_SEC
  int "How long pigeon_shadow_get() waits for the first retained target"
  default 15
  help
    On this connector the shadow is pushed, not fetched, so the first
    pigeon_shadow_get() after a connect has to wait for the retained
    pigeon/shadow/target to arrive (SUBACK then the retained publish,
    normally well under a second). Later calls return the last value
    immediately and never wait. A timeout is reported as -EAGAIN: the
    session is fine, the value has not arrived yet.

config PIGEON_MQTT_PUBLISH_TIMEOUT_SEC
  int "Deadline for one QoS 1 publish, redeliveries included"
  default 30
  help
    Bounds the whole call, not one attempt: a QoS 1 publish interrupted by
    a dropped session is republished with the duplicate flag set once the
    worker reconnects, until this deadline passes. Zephyr's MQTT client
    never retransmits on its own, so this connector owns redelivery.

config PIGEON_MQTT_MAX_INFLIGHT
  int "Concurrent QoS 1 publishes this connector tracks"
  default 2
  range 1 16
  help
    One slot per thread that can be publishing at once. Two covers the
    normal shape -- the application's own thread flushing telemetry or
    reporting a shadow, plus the system workqueue draining
    CONFIG_PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD -- and an oversubscribed store answers
    -EBUSY rather than queueing. Stays under the broker's own in-flight
    ceiling (16 packets, Receive Maximum on MQTT 5).

config PIGEON_MQTT_RECONNECT_MAX_DELAY_SEC
  int "Reconnect backoff ceiling (seconds)"
  default 300

config PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_BACKOFF_SEC
  int "Backoff after the broker refuses this device's credentials"
  default 900
  help
    A refusal that names the credentials (bad username or password, not
    authorized) will answer identically to every retry until something
    changes off-device -- a rotated token, a re-provisioned PSK, a
    deleted pigeon -- so reconnecting on the ordinary schedule is a
    pointless load on the broker and on this device's radio. The session
    still retries, just at this interval rather than seconds.

endif # PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT

if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD

config PIGEON_NIDD_CLAIM_KEY
  string "Claim key, 32 lowercase hex characters"
  default ""
  help
    The pigeon's claim key as the dashboard reveals it, once, at create or token refresh.
    Sent in HELLO at every boot; verifies the tag on every platform frame. Keep it in a
    git-ignored conf file. The build fails unless it is exactly 32 characters.

config PIGEON_NIDD_DEDICATED_CID
  bool "Carry Non-IP on a PDP context of its own"
  default y
  help
    Non-IP on a new context, IP left on the default one, which firmware download needs.
    Off only on a NIDD-only plan: the default context becomes Non-IP, the device has no IP
    at all, and CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA cannot be enabled.

config PIGEON_NIDD_RAI_IDLE_MS
  int "Quiet time after an owed reply before releasing the radio (ms)"
  default 500
  range 0 5000

config PIGEON_NIDD_REPLY_WAIT_SEC
  int "How long pigeon_shadow_report() waits for its confirmation (seconds)"
  default 30
  range 1 300

config PIGEON_NIDD_SHADOW_WAIT_SEC
  int "How long the first pigeon_shadow_get() waits for the reply to HELLO (seconds)"
  default 60
  range 1 600

config PIGEON_NIDD_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
  int "Receive thread stack size"
  default 2048

endif # PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD

config PIGEON_SHADOW_CONFIG_MAX
  int "Max shadow target_config/current_config size (bytes, incl. NUL)"
  default 320
  range 64 4096
  help
    Cap on one decoded shadow config's raw JSON text, shared by the HTTPS
    connector's shadow GET decode, pigeon_shadow_report()'s body framing,
    and (CONFIG_PIGEON_WS) the shadow_update frame decode. The default is
    the historical built-in value, sized for a target_config of a few
    short app keys plus a "firmware" FOTA target. Raise it when the app's
    target_config grows past that (a truncated config fails JSON parsing
    downstream, it doesn't degrade gracefully). Static RAM cost scales
    with it: the HTTPS connector keeps two receive/accumulate buffers
    sized ~2.5x this value each (see PIGEON_HTTPS_RECV_BUF_LEN in
    pigeon_https.c for the arithmetic), plus per-call stack framing of
    about 1x. CONFIG_PIGEON_WS users raising this should check
    CONFIG_PIGEON_WS_RX_BUF_SIZE still covers a full shadow_update frame
    (two escaped configs plus framing).

config PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD
  bool "Remote log upload (Zephyr dictionary-based logging)"
  default n
  depends on PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT
  select LOG_DICTIONARY_SUPPORT
  help
    Capture this device's log output via Zephyr's dictionary-based
    logging (CONFIG_LOG_DICTIONARY_SUPPORT -- the records carry only
    a source id, level, timestamp, and packed arguments, never the
    format strings themselves, which is the low-data win) into a
    bounded ring buffer, and flush it in batches as a raw binary
    POST to <CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT>/logs, device-authenticated the
    same way as this connector's telemetry/shadow reports. Off by
    default -- existing samples build unchanged, and enabling it
    changes the build's log_dictionary.json requirement for decoding
    (see pigeon-examples' README for the host-side decode flow).

    Selecting this does NOT turn the console binary: dictionary
    encoding is per-backend, and only this backend uses it. A UART
    console keeps rendering human-readable text unless the image
    also sets CONFIG_LOG_BACKEND_UART_OUTPUT_DICTIONARY_HEX.

    Both connectors implement the upload. On CoAP a batch larger
    than one datagram's worth is sent as an RFC 7959 Block1
    sequence, so CONFIG_PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD_BUF_SIZE is not capped by
    the CoAP frame size.

    Requires a log mode that dispatches to backends, i.e. NOT
    CONFIG_LOG_MODE_MINIMAL (which never calls a backend at all, so
    the ring buffer would simply stay empty). Prefer
    CONFIG_LOG_MODE_DEFERRED over CONFIG_LOG_MODE_IMMEDIATE: the
    ring buffer is filled by the backend and drained by the flush
    work item, which is a safe single-producer/single-consumer pair
    only while one thread does the filling. Deferred mode guarantees
    that (the log processing thread is the sole producer); immediate
    mode runs the backend on whichever thread happened to log, so
    two of them can race inside the same ring buffer.

if PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD

config PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD_BUF_SIZE
  int "Pigeon log upload ring buffer size (bytes)"
  default 2048
  help
    Bounded ring buffer holding dictionary-mode log records not yet
    uploaded. Once full, newly produced records are dropped rather
    than blocking the caller or growing without bound -- see
    pigeon_log_backend.c.

config PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD_BATCH_SIZE
  int "Pigeon log upload batch flush threshold (bytes)"
  default 512
  range 1 PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD_BUF_SIZE
  help
    Once this many bytes have accumulated in the ring buffer, a
    flush is triggered immediately rather than waiting for the
    max-interval timeout below.

config PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD_MAX_INTERVAL_MS
  int "Pigeon log upload max flush interval (ms)"
  default 60000
  help
    Upper bound on how long a partially-filled batch waits before
    being flushed anyway, so low log-volume devices still ship
    records instead of holding them indefinitely below the
    batch-size threshold above.

endif # PIGEON_LOG_UPLOAD

config PIGEON_FOTA
  bool "Firmware-over-the-air (FOTA) updates via MCUboot"
  default n
  depends on PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT || \
             (PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD && PIGEON_NIDD_DEDICATED_CID)
  depends on BOOTLOADER_MCUBOOT
  depends on IMG_MANAGER
  select HTTP_CLIENT
  select PSA_CRYPTO
  select PSA_WANT_ALG_SHA_256
  select IMG_ERASE_PROGRESSIVELY if !PIGEON_FOTA_NCS
  help
    Chunked HTTP Range-GET download of the firmware image named in the
    shadow's target_config "firmware" key (see capsules/dovecote's
    shadow-driven FOTA route) straight into MCUboot's secondary slot
    as it streams -- the image (up to ~1-2MB) is never held whole in
    RAM. Verifies the downloaded byte count and a streamed sha256
    against the shadow-declared size/sha256 before scheduling a
    one-time test-swap. Does not reboot itself -- the caller must
    gracefully tear down its own connectivity (e.g.
    lte_lc_power_off()/lte_disconnect()) and call pigeon_reboot() once
    pigeon_fota_apply() returns 0, same convention as the existing
    shadow "reboot": true command. Off by default -- existing samples
    build unchanged, and this can only be turned on for an application
    already built under sysbuild with MCUboot (CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_MCUBOOT,
    CONFIG_IMG_MANAGER -- see https_init's sysbuild.conf).

    The download is always HTTPS, on either connector that supports it: a
    firmware image runs to megabytes, the device route already serves it
    Range-chunked with device-side resume, and every MQTT-capable device
    already has TCP and TLS. An MQTT build therefore keeps the HTTPS
    fetch and names its own https:// base in
    CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA_HTTPS_ENDPOINT below, since CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT
    there names the broker. A NIDD build fetches the same way over the IP
    PDN beside its Non-IP one, which is why it needs
    CONFIG_PIGEON_NIDD_DEDICATED_CID. The CoAP connector has no download
    transport for this yet.

    Default backend is upstream Zephyr's own flash_img/stream_flash +
    boot_request_upgrade() (see PIGEON_FOTA_NCS below for the vendor
    alternative) -- no nRF Connect SDK dependency needed for FOTA on a
    plain vanilla Zephyr build.

    That backend is also why IMG_ERASE_PROGRESSIVELY is selected above.
    flash_img_buffered_write() erases the slot it writes only under that
    symbol (which selects STREAM_FLASH_ERASE in turn), and nothing else on
    the download path erases it; a write to NOR flash can only clear bits,
    so an image written over the remains of an earlier one is the bitwise
    AND of the two and fails its own sha256 check at the end of a
    completed download. A factory-erased slot hides this completely: the
    first update a board ever takes succeeds, and every one after it is
    corrupt. The vendor backend needs no equivalent selection here because
    NCS's DFU_TARGET_STREAM makes it for itself.

    MCUboot's own image signature check is the security boundary for
    firmware authenticity here (this library's sha256 check is only an
    integrity check against transport/flash corruption, not a
    signature) -- production deployments must sign with a real key, not
    the default dev key.

if PIGEON_FOTA

config PIGEON_FOTA_HTTPS_ENDPOINT
  string "HTTPS device base the firmware image is fetched from"
  depends on PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD
  default ""
  help
    This pigeon's own https:// device base, e.g.
    https://api.pidgeiot.com/device/pigeons/<pigeon_id> -- exactly what an
    HTTPS-connector build puts in CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT, and needed as its
    own symbol here because on an MQTT build CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT names
    the broker (mqtts://host:8883), which serves no firmware, and on a NIDD
    build the Non-IP APN (nidd://VZWSCEF).

    Required whenever CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA is on with the MQTT or NIDD
    connector, and enforced at build time rather than at download time: an
    empty value is a BUILD_ASSERT in pigeon_https.c, not a device that gets
    a firmware target and then cannot fetch it. The same build must also
    carry CONFIG_PIGEON_TOKEN, even in MQTT's PSK mode, because the image
    fetch is an ordinary device-authenticated HTTPS request whatever the
    connector itself uses.

config PIGEON_FOTA_NCS
  bool "Use the nRF Connect SDK dfu_target backend instead of upstream flash_img"
  default n
  select DFU_TARGET
  select DFU_TARGET_MCUBOOT
  help
    Swaps pigeon_fota.c's flash-write/schedule-update backend from
    upstream Zephyr's flash_img_context + boot_request_upgrade() to
    nRF Connect SDK's dfu_target/dfu_target_mcuboot -- functionally
    equivalent (dfu_target_mcuboot itself is a thin wrapper over the
    same flash_img/boot_request_upgrade primitives underneath), kept
    around as the vendor-SDK path because it's what real nRF91
    hardware e2e was actually verified against (see this library's
    CLAUDE.md), even though the default favors the vendor-neutral
    upstream path instead. Only meaningful -- and only
    buildable -- under an nRF Connect SDK west workspace (west.yml,
    not west-vanilla.yml, in the sibling pigeon-examples repo), since
    DFU_TARGET/DFU_TARGET_MCUBOOT are defined by the nrf module, not
    plain Zephyr.

config PIGEON_FOTA_RESUME
  bool "Persist FOTA download progress across attempts and reboots"
  default y
  depends on SETTINGS
  depends on !SETTINGS_NONE
  select STREAM_FLASH_PROGRESS if !PIGEON_FOTA_NCS
  select DFU_TARGET_STREAM_SAVE_PROGRESS if PIGEON_FOTA_NCS
  help
    Without this option, any failed or interrupted pigeon_fota_apply()
    restarts from byte 0 -- on an LTE-M departure sign, a download that
    died at 72% of a ~486KB image threw away ~45 minutes of Range-GET
    progress, on a link that itself degrades under sustained traffic.
    With it, the number of bytes
    confirmed FLUSHED to the MCUboot secondary slot is persisted (via the
    settings subsystem, alongside the flash-write backend's own persisted
    stream progress) after every chunk, and the next pigeon_fota_apply()
    call -- same boot or after a reboot -- re-hashes the already-flushed
    slot bytes to rebuild the streamed sha256 (seconds of flash reads;
    no crypto context is ever serialized) and re-requests the remainder
    via HTTP Range from where the flash actually stopped, never from a
    bytes-received offset whose RAM-buffered tail a reboot would have
    discarded.

    Persisted progress is invalidated (next download starts clean from
    byte 0) whenever the shadow's firmware target version changes,
    whenever the completion sha256 verify fails (a resumed-but-corrupt
    image must not poison the following attempt), and whenever the
    backend's restored offset can't belong to the current target (e.g.
    it exceeds the declared image size, or no record vouches for it).

    ATTEMPT SEMANTICS: PIGEON_FOTA_ATTEMPT_BUDGET, if enabled, does not
    charge a resumed continuation that moves the flushed offset forward --
    resuming is meant to be cheap, and a budget one dropped connection
    could spend would be exhausted by the very conditions this option
    exists to survive. A continuation that ends where it began is charged
    like a fresh attempt, so a transfer stuck at a fixed offset still
    terminates. An application counting attempts itself sees one
    pigeon_fota_apply() call as one attempt regardless, and should
    consider adopting the library budget instead of hand-rolling this
    distinction.

    Requires the application to provide a working settings backend
    (CONFIG_SETTINGS plus e.g. CONFIG_NVS and a storage partition); this
    library only reads/writes its own "pigeon/fota" settings keys. On the
    CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA_NCS backend this also enables NCS's own
    CONFIG_DFU_TARGET_STREAM_SAVE_PROGRESS, whose default flips
    CONFIG_DFU_TARGET_STREAM_SYNCHRONOUS on -- every chunk is then
    flushed to flash before the next Range GET is issued, which is
    exactly the granularity resume wants.

    On by default. It started off purely so that adding it changed no
    existing build, not because it was ever found unreliable; what it
    guards against is expensive and routine on a metered or marginal link,
    and starting a download over from byte 0 is the more surprising
    behavior to inherit by default. Builds without a settings backend keep
    the old behavior automatically, since this cannot select one for them
    (see the dependency above). Turn it off to shed the persisted state
    entirely -- at the price of every interruption costing the whole
    transfer again.

config PIGEON_FOTA_CHUNK_SIZE
  int "FOTA download chunk size (bytes)"
  default 2048
  help
    Size of each Range-GET request issued against
    <CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT>/firmware, and of the matching static
    receive/staging buffers this allocates. Each chunk is written
    straight to flash as it arrives (see pigeon_fota_apply() in
    pigeon_fota.c) -- the full image is never buffered whole in RAM,
    only this window of it.

config PIGEON_FOTA_CHUNK_YIELD_MS
  int "Pause between FOTA chunk downloads (ms)"
  default 0
  range 0 1000
  help
    Sleep this long between consecutive Range-GET chunk requests during
    pigeon_fota_apply(). 0 (the default) fetches chunks back to back,
    which on a narrow link (LTE-M) can saturate the modem's downlink and
    DNS/socket capacity so thoroughly that the application's own
    concurrent traffic starves -- on an nRF9160 departure sign, the app's
    30s fetch cycle failed repeatedly for the whole download. A modest
    yield (tens of ms)
    opens periodic airtime windows for the rest of the system at a small
    cost in total download time (chunks x yield). Tune per-fleet; the
    right value depends on link bandwidth and how chatty the app is.

config PIGEON_FOTA_CHUNK_RETRIES
  int "Consecutive transient failures tolerated per chunk"
  default 3
  range 0 100
  help
    How many times a single Range-GET may fail and be retried at the same
    offset before pigeon_fota_apply() gives up on the transfer. The count
    is consecutive and resets on every chunk that lands, so a long download
    over a link that hiccups periodically is never penalized for its
    length -- only a transfer genuinely stuck at one offset runs the budget
    out.

    Retries cover the network step alone. Nothing has been written to flash
    or folded into the running sha256 at that point, so re-requesting the
    same bytes is safe; a failure after the write (the stream is
    append-only, with no way to un-write a partial chunk) still ends the
    call, to be picked up by PIGEON_FOTA_RESUME on the next one.

    0 restores the previous behavior, where the first failed chunk ended
    the whole transfer.

config PIGEON_FOTA_RATE_LIMIT_RETRIES
  int "Consecutive rate-limit pauses tolerated per chunk"
  default 10
  range 0 100
  help
    How many times pigeon_fota_apply() will wait out an HTTP 429 at the
    same offset before giving up. Separate from
    PIGEON_FOTA_CHUNK_RETRIES on purpose: a 429 is the server pacing the
    transfer, not failing it, and must not be able to spend the budget
    reserved for real errors -- otherwise a fleet-wide rate limit would
    read to operators as every device failing its download.

    Each pause is the response's own Retry-After (clamped by
    PIGEON_FOTA_RETRY_AFTER_MAX_SEC), or a short default when the server
    sent none. The counter resets on every chunk that lands.

config PIGEON_FOTA_RETRY_AFTER_MAX_SEC
  int "Ceiling on a single download backoff (seconds)"
  default 60
  range 1 3600
  help
    Clamps both a server-supplied Retry-After and this library's own
    exponential retry backoff. A server (or a misconfigured proxy in front
    of one) asking for an hour must not be able to park a device inside
    pigeon_fota_apply() for an hour.

    Worth checking against the application's watchdog before raising:
    pigeon_fota_apply() blocks its calling thread for the whole download
    already, and each wait adds to that. On a device whose watchdog is fed
    from the same thread, this value bounds one more contribution to the
    longest stretch between feeds.

config PIGEON_FOTA_ATTEMPT_BUDGET
  bool "Bound how many times one firmware target may be attempted"
  default n
  depends on SETTINGS
  depends on !SETTINGS_NONE
  help
    Counts attempts at each firmware target, persistently, and stops once
    the budget below is spent -- so an image that downloads and verifies
    cleanly but then boot-loops until MCUboot reverts it cannot re-download
    itself on every shadow poll forever.

    The count is bound to the shadow target_version that named the
    firmware, not to the version string alone, which is what makes it
    recoverable. The platform advances target_version when target_config
    changes, so a shadow write that keeps the same firmware target and
    changes something else reopens the budget. Writing a byte-identical
    target_config back does not reopen it: the version moves on a change,
    not on a write. The dashboard's "Re-push firmware" action supplies that
    change for the operator, leaving the firmware target itself untouched.
    Without a reopen path, a handful of unlucky timeouts would retire a
    firmware version on that device permanently, and the only way back
    would be republishing identical bytes under a new version string.
    Nothing on the device can fake the signal: target_version comes from
    the platform, and rebooting or re-polling leaves it unchanged.

    What one attempt means is defined in pigeon_fota_apply(): a download
    starting from byte 0 spends one, while a PIGEON_FOTA_RESUME
    continuation spends nothing so long as it moves the flushed offset
    forward -- resuming is meant to be cheap, and charging for it would
    make an interrupted transfer eat the budget in the exact conditions
    resume exists to survive. A continuation that ends where it started
    spends one, so a transfer wedged at a fixed offset still terminates.

    Off by default. An application that already keeps its own attempt
    counter (as the departure-board firmware does) would otherwise end up
    with two independent budgets counting the same downloads; adopting this
    one is a deliberate switch, paired with deleting the local copy. See
    pigeon_fota_attempt_allowed()/pigeon_fota_attempts_clear() in pigeon.h
    for the two calls an application needs.

if PIGEON_FOTA_ATTEMPT_BUDGET

config PIGEON_FOTA_MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_VERSION
  int "Attempts allowed per firmware target"
  default 3
  range 1 255
  help
    How many attempts one (firmware version, shadow target_version) pair
    gets before pigeon_fota_attempt_allowed() starts answering false.

    Low is fine, and lower than it would be without a recovery path: the
    cost of exhausting the budget is no longer permanent, so this can be
    tuned for "stop wasting the link" rather than for "never give up".

endif # PIGEON_FOTA_ATTEMPT_BUDGET

config PIGEON_FOTA_CURRENT_VERSION
  string "Currently running firmware version string"
  help
    Compared byte-for-byte against the shadow target_config's
    firmware.version field (see pigeon_fota_update_available()) to
    decide whether a FOTA download is needed. This library does not
    read MCUboot's own embedded image version -- it trusts this
    build-time string, so bump it deliberately alongside whatever
    version string the corresponding image gets tagged with on the
    platform side (ideally from the same release pipeline), or the
    device will either loop re-downloading its own already-running
    image or never converge.

endif # PIGEON_FOTA

config PIGEON_WS
  bool "WebSocket push channel (persistent connection to <endpoint>/ws)"
  default n
  depends on PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS
  select WEBSOCKET_CLIENT
  select NET_CONTEXT_SNDTIMEO
  help
    Persistent wss:// connection to <CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT>/ws (dovecote's
    device WebSocket endpoint). Server pushes shadow_update frames
    on dashboard config writes so the device reacts without polling; the
    device sends application-level ping keepalives and (opportunistically)
    telemetry frames over the open socket. HTTPS remains the transport for
    shadow GET/report-back, log upload, and FOTA -- WS is push + telemetry
    only. Reuses CONFIG_PIGEON_ENDPOINT / CONFIG_PIGEON_TOKEN /
    CONFIG_PIGEON_HTTPS_SEC_TAG; no separate WS credentials exist.
    Zephyr's WEBSOCKET_CLIENT is marked EXPERIMENTAL upstream.
    NET_CONTEXT_SNDTIMEO is required for SO_SNDTIMEO (set on the WS socket
    in pigeon_ws_open_tls_socket()) to have any effect -- without it, every
    blocking send on this socket, including Zephyr's own internal CLOSE-frame
    echo, defaults to an unbounded wait (see that function's comment).

if PIGEON_WS

config PIGEON_WS_RX_BUF_SIZE
  int "WS receive/scratch buffer size (bytes)"
  default 1024
  help
    Sizes both websocket_request.tmp_buf (HTTP upgrade response + frame
    parse scratch, must outlive the connection) and the frame reassembly
    buffer a shadow_update must fit into. A shadow_update carries the full
    PigeonShadow with two escaped JSON-string configs; the HTTPS connector
    caps each decoded config at PIGEON_HTTPS_CONFIG_MAX (320), so 1024
    covers the framed/escaped equivalent. Server-side hard cap per frame
    is 16384 (dovecote MAX_WS_FRAME_BYTES); oversized inbound frames are
    drained and dropped with a warning, not fatal.

config PIGEON_WS_PING_INTERVAL_SEC
  int "Keepalive ping interval (seconds)"
  default 60
  help
    The server NEVER pings (dovecote objects/ws.rs: WsOutboundFrame::Ping
    is dead code); the device owns keepalive. Each interval sends
    {"type":"ping"} and expects {"type":"pong"}. Two consecutive missed
    pongs mark the connection half-open and force a reconnect. Budget:
    well under the server's 50-frames/10s limit.

config PIGEON_WS_RECONNECT_MAX_DELAY_SEC
  int "Reconnect backoff ceiling (seconds)"
  default 300

config PIGEON_WS_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
  int "WS worker thread stack size (bytes)"
  default 8192
  help
    The worker thread performs the TLS handshake (mbedTLS) itself; 8192
    matches wifi_init's TLS-proven CONFIG_MAIN_STACK_SIZE precedent.

endif # PIGEON_WS

config PIGEON_SHELL
  bool "Remote diagnostic shell over the device WebSocket channel"
  default n
  depends on PIGEON_WS
  select SHELL
  select SHELL_BACKEND_DUMMY
  help
    Lets an operator run a small, owner-configured allowlist of diagnostic
    shell commands on this device remotely, relayed over the existing
    CONFIG_PIGEON_WS socket. This is remote code execution on
    the device by design -- off by default, and even once enabled, denies
    every command unless it matches CONFIG_PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOWLIST (or
    CONFIG_PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOW_ALL is explicitly set). Only enable for
    fleets where an operator-triggered diagnostic shell is a deliberate,
    reviewed choice -- see pigeon's CLAUDE.md for the full design writeup.
    Commands run on a dedicated thread, never inline on the WS worker
    thread, so a hung command can't stall pings/reconnect (it can still
    wedge the device itself until reboot -- there is no command
    cancellation in v1, which is exactly why the allowlist should only
    ever contain fast, bounded, read-mostly diagnostics).

if PIGEON_SHELL

config PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOWLIST
  string "Comma-separated allowed command prefixes"
  default ""
  help
    A command is permitted only if it matches one of these prefixes
    exactly, or the prefix followed by a space (so "pigeon shadow" allows
    "pigeon shadow" and "pigeon shadow foo", but not "pigeon shadowfoo").
    Checked in pigeon_shell.c before shell_execute_cmd() is ever called --
    deliberately not relying on "don't enable dangerous shell Kconfig
    options" as the sole defense, since that posture silently breaks the
    next time an unrelated shell-producing Kconfig option gets turned on
    for a different reason. Left empty (the default): every command is
    denied, since CONFIG_PIGEON_SHELL alone only wires up the transport,
    not any permitted commands -- the device owner must deliberately
    populate this list (or set CONFIG_PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOW_ALL) before this
    feature can execute anything at all. Example:
    "pigeon version,pigeon shadow,pigeon ws status".

config PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOW_ALL
  bool "Allow ALL shell commands (disables command filtering)"
  default n
  help
    WARNING: this disables CONFIG_PIGEON_SHELL_ALLOWLIST entirely and lets
    the operator dashboard run ANY command compiled into this device's
    shell, including anything a driver/subsystem's own shell module
    happens to register (kernel introspection, flash/devmem pokes,
    reboot, etc.) -- whatever that surface turns out to be, present and
    future, as this build's Kconfig changes over time. This is an
    explicit escape hatch for a device owner who has reviewed their own
    firmware's shell command surface and deliberately wants full
    flexibility with no on-device filtering -- "their device, their
    rules." Leave off (the default) unless you have made that call
    knowingly.

config PIGEON_SHELL_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
  int "Shell command execution thread stack size (bytes)"
  default 4096
  help
    Dedicated thread so a hung/slow shell_execute_cmd() call can never
    stall the CONFIG_PIGEON_WS worker thread's ping/reconnect loop --
    see this option's parent help text. Fed by a depth-1 k_msgq: v1 is
    one command in flight per device by design, a second shell_cmd
    arriving mid-execution gets an immediate "busy" shell_output reply
    rather than being queued or silently dropped.

endif # PIGEON_SHELL

config PIGEON_REBOOT_ON_FATAL
  bool "Reboot instead of hanging forever on a Zephyr fatal error"
  default n
  help
    The PRIMARY mitigation for the exact failure diagnosed below (see
    CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG for the full trace): real ESP32-C6 hardware
    hit a Zephyr FATAL error (z_fatal_error, an
    "Environment call from M-mode" -- a deliberate ecall-based assert deep
    inside Espressif's closed-source WiFi MAC blob, not a wild memory fault)
    after 42-60 minutes of uptime. Zephyr's default policy for ANY fatal
    error (k_sys_fatal_error_handler -> arch_system_halt(), see
    zephyr/kernel/fatal.c) is to lock interrupts and spin forever --
    confirmed on real hardware as total serial silence and zero response to
    shell input, for over an hour, on both reproductions.

    k_sys_fatal_error_handler() is documented and declared as an
    application-overridable weak symbol for exactly this kind of policy
    change (see zephyr/include/zephyr/fatal.h's own doc comment). This
    option supplies that override: flush pending log output (LOG_PANIC(),
    per that same doc comment's guidance for an unrecoverable error) and
    call pigeon_reboot() instead of halting -- converting an eternal hang
    into a deterministic, immediate self-reboot, entirely in software, from
    the fault context itself, with no dependency on any watchdog (task or
    hardware) ever needing to notice and fire. This is a headless,
    unattended device library -- an operator has no console to recover a
    hung unit from, so "reboot and try again" is the right default posture
    for ANY fatal error here, even though it necessarily discards whatever
    a real debugger session might otherwise inspect at the fault site --
    fine for a fleet device, not what you want while actively debugging on
    a dev board (leave this off for that).

    Complementary to, not a replacement for, CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG below:
    this hook only runs for an error Zephyr's own fault handler recognizes
    and calls out for (a real panic/assert/CPU fault) -- it does nothing
    for a silent liveness stall where the kernel keeps scheduling normally
    but application-level progress just stops (no fatal error ever fires).
    That gap is what CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG's task-watchdog channel covers.

config PIGEON_WATCHDOG
  bool "Wedge-recovery watchdog (reboot on stalled liveness)"
  default n
  select TASK_WDT
  select WATCHDOG
  help
    Real ESP32-C6 soak testing found this library's WS/HTTPS stack can
    hard-wedge after 42-60 minutes of uptime -- a rare, reconnect-
    churn-triggered assert deep inside Espressif's closed-source WiFi MAC
    blob (libnet80211.a, no source available -- see this library's CLAUDE.md
    for the symbol-resolved trace), which trips Zephyr's OWN fatal-error
    path (z_fatal_error -> k_sys_fatal_error_handler, an "Environment call
    from M-mode" -- a deliberate ecall, not a wild memory fault) rather than
    anything pigeon or the app did wrong. Two soaks confirmed the arena
    the crash traces into (CONFIG_COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA) never even gets
    touched under normal operation, ruling out "just size something bigger"
    -- this is a latent vendor bug, not a resource-exhaustion bug, and the
    only available fix on this side is detecting the wedge and recovering
    from it.

    This option's job is narrower than CONFIG_PIGEON_REBOOT_ON_FATAL above,
    which is the PRIMARY mitigation for the exact bug just described (a
    real Zephyr fatal error, caught deterministically by that hook). This
    option instead covers a DIFFERENT failure shape: a silent liveness
    stall where the kernel itself keeps scheduling fine, but application-
    level progress (WS pongs, telemetry/shadow round trips) just stops --
    no fatal error ever fires, so the fatal-hook above never runs. It wraps
    Zephyr's own Task Watchdog subsystem (CONFIG_TASK_WDT) rather than
    inventing a bespoke mechanism: one channel, fed by confirmed round-trip
    liveness (every successful pigeon_shadow_flush() telemetry report over
    whichever transport is active, plus -- when CONFIG_PIGEON_WS is also on
    -- every WS pong received, a higher-frequency heartbeat independent of
    whatever telemetry_interval an operator has configured). If the channel
    isn't fed within CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SEC, the channel's
    callback (pigeon_watchdog_expired()) logs the expiry and reboots
    through pigeon_reboot().

    CONFIG_TASK_WDT_HW_FALLBACK (on by default) is ALSO meant to be a
    backstop for the interrupts-disabled case CONFIG_PIGEON_REBOOT_ON_FATAL
    already handles via a cleaner path -- a genuine hardware timer,
    independent of the CPU's interrupt state, forcing a full SoC reset once
    its own window (CONFIG_TASK_WDT_MIN_TIMEOUT + CONFIG_TASK_WDT_HW_
    FALLBACK_DELAY) expires with nobody left alive to keep feeding it.
    **Known-broken on ESP32 targets**: zephyr/drivers/watchdog/wdt_esp32.c
    passes the millisecond timeout value straight into wdt_hal_config_stage()
    as raw hardware TICKS with no conversion (that HAL function's own doc
    says its parameter is ticks; this driver defines the conversion
    constants it would need, MWDT_TICK_PRESCALER/MWDT_TICKS_PER_US, but
    never uses them -- confirmed by comparison against ESP-IDF's own
    reference task-watchdog code in the same vendored tree, which does
    convert). A deliberately forced interrupts-disabled hang
    (arch_irq_lock(); for(;;){}) on real ESP32-C6 hardware did not recover:
    zero bytes on live serial for 550+ seconds (~9.2 minutes) against an
    intended ~120ms deadline -- roughly 4500x over, with no reset observed.
    Draft upstream report: pigeon-examples' docs/upstream-issues/
    zephyr-wdt_esp32-missing-tick-conversion.md. Deliberately NOT working
    around this here (e.g. with a compensating ms->ticks fudge factor) --
    that would silently double-convert the day upstream fixes the driver,
    and CONFIG_PIGEON_REBOOT_ON_FATAL already covers the failure this
    project actually diagnosed, making the hardware-fallback gap an honest,
    documented residual risk rather than a blocking one.

    Vendor-neutral by construction, not an ESP32-specific option otherwise:
    the hardware watchdog is looked up via this board's own `watchdog0`
    devicetree alias (a standard Zephyr convention many boards implement,
    not something this library selects a vendor driver for), so the
    software-channel behavior (this option's actual job) benefits any
    board/transport combination that can wedge the same way, not just
    ESP32-C6 over WS. A board with no `watchdog0` alias still gets the
    software-only task watchdog (logged as a reduced-guarantee warning at
    start) rather than a build failure.

if PIGEON_WATCHDOG

config PIGEON_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SEC
  int "Wedge timeout before a forced reboot (seconds)"
  default 900
  help
    How long this library can go without a confirmed liveness feed (see
    the parent option's help) before treating the device as wedged.
    Deliberately generous, not tight: CONFIG_PIGEON_WS_RECONNECT_MAX_DELAY_SEC
    defaults to 300s, and a real-but-recovering outage (AP down for a few
    minutes, several WiFi join retries) can legitimately go several backoff
    cycles without a single successful pong or telemetry flush -- too short
    a timeout here would reboot a device that was never actually wedged,
    potentially into a reboot loop under ordinary network flakiness. 900s
    (15 minutes) is comfortably above that worst-case healthy-reconnect
    gap, while still well under the ~42-60 minute wedge window both real-
    hardware soaks observed -- the device recovers on its own well before
    an operator would notice via any external monitoring/alerting lag.
    Tune per-fleet if a different margin makes sense; this default is a
    reasoned starting point, not a measured optimum.

endif # PIGEON_WATCHDOG

config PIGEON_RESET_CAUSE_TELEMETRY
  bool "Report the boot's reset cause as telemetry"
  default y
  select HWINFO
  help
    Reads Zephyr's hwinfo reset-cause register once in pigeon_init() and
    queues it as a "reset_cause" telemetry key -- the raw hwinfo bitmask
    rather than a decoded string, so it stays a numeric value: graphable
    in fancier and forwarded over line protocol like any other numeric
    key, instead of only rendering as a chip (see the telemetry-
    forwarding note in pidgeiot's CLAUDE.md on why a non-numeric value
    is excluded from both). It rides the first telemetry flush of each
    boot, the same one-key-on-one-report cost CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG's
    liveness feed already pays.

    This closes an observability gap found the hard way: a departure
    board rebooted in production and the cause was never recorded
    anywhere the platform could reach -- only a bench UART capture that
    dropped the boot banner on reset, and a 200-chunk dictionary log
    that has to be pulled and decoded offline. With this on, every
    future reboot is self-explaining from the dashboard the platform
    already has, no forensics required.

    Deliberately read-only: this never calls hwinfo_clear_reset_cause().
    An application that reads and clears the cause itself before calling
    pigeon_init() (embedded-departure-board's own log_reset_reason()
    does exactly this in its boot path) would otherwise make this read
    racy against init ordering -- whichever of the two ran second would
    see an already-cleared register. Leaving it alone means both readers
    always see the same value regardless of which runs first, at the
    cost of never distinguishing this boot's cause from a prior one on a
    platform whose reset-cause register accumulates across resets
    without a clear (most platforms in this fleet report only the most
    recent cause regardless; see hwinfo_esp32.c/hwinfo_nrf.c).

    On by default: a board with no hwinfo backend gets a logged warning
    and no reset_cause key that boot (hwinfo_get_reset_cause() returns
    -ENOSYS via Zephyr's own weak default, see hwinfo_weak_impl.c), not
    a build failure, so this is safe to leave on fleet-wide.

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH
  bool "Accumulate telemetry readings and deliver them as one batched report"
  default n
  depends on PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS || PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD
  help
    Turns each pigeon_telemetry_flush() into "close the pending keys into
    one timestamped reading, then deliver the accumulated readings only
    when a batch is due" instead of "send one report now". The delivered
    body is the platform's batched form,
    {"reports":[{"age_secs":N,"metrics":{...}},...]} (dovecote's
    POST <endpoint>/telemetry, and the same shape inside a WS telemetry
    frame), rather than the flat single-reading object.

    What this buys, and who it buys it for: a delivery costs the platform
    one worker request, one auth hop, one queue message, one Durable
    Object round trip and one history write no matter how many readings
    it carries, while the billable message count follows the readings
    themselves. Six readings in one delivery are therefore about 3.4x
    cheaper to serve than six deliveries, and bill the account exactly
    what six separate reports would have. This is a cost-of-goods
    change, not a customer discount -- and it is worth turning on only
    for a device chatty enough for the arithmetic to matter.

    It also buys the device something: readings accumulated while the
    link is down are delivered afterwards with the age they were taken
    at, so an outage leaves a gap-free series rather than a hole. The
    ages are k_uptime_get() deltas -- this library has no wall clock, and
    that is exactly why the platform's wire form is "how long ago" rather
    than a timestamp.

    Off by default: batching trades delivery latency for cost, and a
    device reporting on a slow cadence (a 30 s departure sign, say) is
    better served by the unbatched path, whose reading reaches the
    dashboard as soon as it is taken. Leaving this off keeps today's
    behavior exactly -- same flat body, same clear-on-success, same
    per-flush delivery.

    HTTPS and NIDD connectors only for now. On NIDD a batch is how a wake's
    readings leave in one frame, so one radio access. The CoAP terminator
    frames requests differently and its batched shape has not been
    specified yet, so CoAP builds keep the flat per-flush report.

if PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH_DEPTH
  int "Readings per batch, and readings the buffer holds"
  default 6
  range 2 64
  help
    Both the delivery trigger and the buffer's own depth: a batch is due
    once this many readings are buffered, and a reading recorded past
    that drops the oldest one. The two are deliberately the same number,
    since holding more readings than a delivery can carry would only
    guarantee that the surplus is dropped rather than sent.

    The default of 6 is the profile the platform's COGS model was priced
    on -- one reading every 10 s delivered once a minute, which is where
    most of the saving already is (3.4x; the cap of 64 only reaches
    5.9x). 64 is the platform's own per-batch cap
    (capsules::MAX_TELEMETRY_BATCH_READINGS); a batch over it is refused
    whole with 400, so this range cannot be raised past it.

    Note the interaction with the distinct-key cap: the platform refuses
    a batch carrying more than 128 distinct keys across the union of its
    readings. A device reporting the same key set every reading is never
    close to it, which is the normal case; a device that rotates key
    names across readings can reach it with this knob set high, and the
    refusal drops the whole batch (loudly -- see the flush-failure
    handling in pigeon_core.c). Depth x CONFIG_PIGEON_TELEMETRY_MAX_KEYS
    at or under 128 makes that unreachable by construction.

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH_BUF_SIZE
  int "Accumulation arena for buffered readings (bytes)"
  default 1024 if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD
  default 2048
  range 256 8192
  help
    Static byte budget holding the buffered readings' already-escaped
    JSON metrics fragments back to back. This, not the depth above, is
    usually the binding constraint: it must cover DEPTH readings' worth
    of whatever key/value set the application actually reports (five
    short numeric keys run about 100 bytes a reading, so the 2048
    default carries the default depth several times over).

    Sized too small it is not a correctness problem, only a throughput
    one: a batch becomes due once the arena is three quarters full, so
    the buffer spends its capacity on deliveries rather than on drops.
    Readings are only ever dropped -- oldest first, counted, and warned
    about once per flush -- when deliveries themselves are failing and
    new readings keep arriving.

    Total static RAM for this feature is roughly twice this value: the
    arena, plus the assembled body buffer that the transport sends from
    (see PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH_BODY_MAX in
    src/pigeon_telemetry_batch.h). The build fails rather than the
    device 413s if this and the depth together could ever emit a body
    over the platform's 16 KiB batch cap.

    On NIDD the default is 1024, sized so a batch body fits one 1273-byte
    frame at the default depth.

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH_MAX_AGE_SEC
  int "Deliver a partial batch once its oldest reading is this old (seconds)"
  default 60
  range 1 3600
  help
    Upper bound on how long a reading waits for company. Without it a
    device that stops sampling -- or samples more slowly than the depth
    trigger assumes -- would hold its last few readings indefinitely,
    and its connection-state badge on the dashboard would go stale even
    though the device is healthy.

    The default pairs with the default depth: one reading every 10 s
    reaches 6 readings in a minute, so the depth trigger fires first and
    this one only covers a device that went quiet.

    Keep this comfortably under CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SEC on a
    build using CONFIG_PIGEON_WATCHDOG without CONFIG_PIGEON_WS: a
    successful delivery is what feeds that watchdog, and batching is
    what lengthens the gap between deliveries. The build asserts the
    ordering rather than leaving it to be discovered as a reboot loop.

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH_BACKOFF_MAX_SEC
  int "Cap on the retry delay after a failed batch delivery (seconds)"
  default 300
  range 1 3600
  help
    A failed delivery does not consume the buffer: the readings stay,
    and the next flush that would have sent them waits out a backoff
    first -- doubling from 5 s to this cap, with +-25% jitter so a fleet
    sharing an outage does not retry in lockstep and rebuild the spike
    it is backing off from.

    A 429 overrides the schedule with the server's own Retry-After when
    it sends a usable one, clamped to this cap. That is the response a
    free-tier account gets once its pooled message allowance is spent,
    and the platform documents the device as backing off and keeping its
    readings queued -- which is exactly what this does.

    Two failures are not paced but dropped, loudly: 400 and 413 mean the
    batch itself violates a cap (too many readings, too many distinct
    keys, an oversized key, value, or body), and resending those same
    bytes fails identically forever.

endif # PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BATCH

endif # PIGEON

# Not nested under "if PIGEON": pigeon_core.c (which reads these) is compiled
# unconditionally by CMakeLists.txt regardless of CONFIG_PIGEON, so they must
# always have a value (an unsatisfied "if PIGEON" dependency would otherwise
# drop the CONFIG_PIGEON_* macro from autoconf.h entirely).
config PIGEON_ENDPOINT
  string "Pigeon Backend Endpoint"
  help
    URL of the PidgeIoT edge endpoint this device connects to: an
    https:// REST URL for CONFIG_PIGEON_CONNECTOR_HTTPS, or -- for
    CONFIG_PIGEON_CONNECTOR_COAP -- a coaps:// (RFC 7252 CoAP-over-
    DTLS/UDP, CONFIG_PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_UDP) or coaps+tcp:// (RFC 8323
    CoAP-over-TLS/TCP, CONFIG_PIGEON_COAP_TRANSPORT_TCP) host; the scheme
    must match the compiled-in transport (validated at first use). For
    CONFIG_PIGEON_CONNECTOR_MQTT it is the broker itself and carries no
    path at all (mqtts://mqtt.pidgeiot.com:8883): the pigeon is named by
    the handshake, not by a URL, which is also why that connector needs
    pigeon_config.device_id to hold the real pigeon id rather than the
    log-only placeholder the other samples pass.
    For CONFIG_PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD it is nidd://<APN>, the APN of the
    Non-IP PDN (nidd://VZWSCEF on Verizon), checked at pigeon_init().
    Mirrors dovecote's build_http_endpoint output for HTTPS (e.g.
    https://api.pidgeiot.com/device/pigeons/<device_id>) and
    build_coap_endpoint's output for CoAP -- though dovecote has no
    actual CoAP listener deployed yet, only the endpoint string.

config PIGEON_TOKEN
  string "Pigeon Device Auth Token"
  help
    The opaque per-pigeon bearer credential issued by the backend (see
    dovecote's mint_device_credential/get_shadow_device -- a compact binary
    token verified against this pigeon's own stored public key, not a JWT),
    with no "Bearer " prefix -- the HTTPS connector adds that itself when
    building the Authorization header. Returned once at provisioning (or
    re-minted, invalidating the previous token, via a token/refresh call);
    override this per build (e.g. via a board/app Kconfig fragment) rather
    than committing a real token here. Read by the HTTPS connector (the
    Authorization header) and by CONFIG_PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_CERT (the CONNECT
    password). A NIDD build reads it only for CONFIG_PIGEON_FOTA, whose
    image fetch is an HTTPS request over the IP PDN. The connectors that
    authenticate through a PSK handshake instead -- CoAP, and
    CONFIG_PIGEON_MQTT_AUTH_PSK -- never read it: the platform maps the
    handshake identity to the pigeon and holds the token server-side, so
    those builds may leave it empty.

config PIGEON_TELEMETRY_MAX_KEYS
  int "Max distinct telemetry keys pending between flushes"
  default 7 if PIGEON_CONNECTOR_NIDD
  default 8
  range 1 64
  help
    Number of key/value slots in the batched pending-telemetry store
    (pigeon_telemetry_set()). One pigeon_telemetry_flush() sends every
    pending key in a single flat JSON report -- one HTTPS/CoAP POST or
    one WS telemetry frame -- rather than one request per key, which is
    what makes an N-key report cycle cost one radio round trip instead
    of N. Setting an already-pending key overwrites its value in place
    (latest-value-per-key, matching the backend's own store semantics);
    setting a NEW key while every slot holds a distinct pending key
    fails with -ENOMEM until a flush drains the store. Static RAM cost
    scales linearly: ~160 bytes/slot for the store plus ~165 bytes/slot
    for the shared flush body buffer (see PIGEON_TELEMETRY_BODY_MAX in
    pigeon_internal.h) -- and the CoAP connector's frame buffers scale
    with it too (see PIGEON_COAP_MSG_MAX in pigeon_coap.c). NIDD defaults
    to 7: eight worst-case keys make a 1323-byte body, over one NIDD frame.

config PIGEON_LOG_LEVEL
  int "Pigeon Library Log Level"
  default 3
  range 0 4
  help
    0: None, 1: Error, 2: Warning, 3: Info, 4: Debug
